# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: encoding-replacement-character-option -- an option to substitute unencodable characters instead of signalling
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: feat-encoding-replacement-character-option
#+DATE: 2026-11-03

* Symptom

Found while running the error-processing catalogue probe
(error-processing-per-dialect.issue) under clautolisp 2.3.81. The probe
logs every result with

#+begin_src lisp
(setq f (open log "a"))            ; default write encoding: windows-1252
(write-line (vl-prin1-to-string res) f)
#+end_src

Case 102 is =(chr 300)= (LATIN CAPITAL LETTER I WITH BREVE, absent from
windows-1252). Its printed value went to WRITE-LINE, and clautolisp aborted
the whole run. The error escaped to =*error*=:

: AutoLISP builtin WRITE-LINE signaled an error: :CP1252 stream encoding error on
:   #<SB-SYS:FD-STREAM for "file .../catalogue-log.txt">: the character with code 300 cannot be encoded.

The three vendors ran the same probe to the end. Their logs show what each
one does with the character, but only after vl-prin1-to-string, so this is
indirect evidence:

| product                    | (vl-prin1-to-string (chr 300)) as logged |
|----------------------------+------------------------------------------|
| AutoCAD 2022 accoreconsole | "," |
| BricsCAD V25 Windows       | "I" |
| BricsCAD V26 macOS         | "\U+012C" |

Measured directly by round-trip cases 535-545 (below).

* Request (pjb, 2026-11-03)

An OPTION that chooses what happens when a character cannot be encoded in
the target encoding, instead of the hard-wired signal:

- =-Ereplacement CHAR= / =--encoding-replacement CHAR=: substitute CHAR
  (e.g. =?=), with an =error= value for today's behaviour. It could also
  be a per-situation form, like the other =-E= options:
  =-Efile-write-replacement=, =-Eterminal-out-replacement=.
- Surfaced to user code as a setq-able global, following the existing
  =*AUTOLISP-FILE-WRITE-ENCODING*= naming:
  =*AUTOLISP-ENCODING-REPLACEMENT*= (a one-character string, or nil = signal).
- Applies to every stream clautolisp opens on the user's behalf when no
  explicit external format says otherwise: OPEN without an encoding
  argument, the terminal streams, and alfe's console capture.
  An explicit ="(:utf-8 :replacement #\?)"= third argument
  (parse-open-external-format, autolisp-builtins-core api.lisp:2103)
  keeps its precedence.
- The default per dialect follows the vendors once measured. If a vendor
  substitutes, its dialect substitutes by default and warns once
  ([encoding-replacement]). Under --strict the default stays to signal.
- Decoding (reading an invalid byte sequence) is a separate question with
  its own measured AutoCAD behaviour (UTF-8 → windows-1252 fallback,
  clautolisp-user-manual.org "Under the AutoCAD dialects, LISPSYS
  decides"). Keep it out of this option, or give it its own
  (=-Efile-read-replacement=).

* Also

Whatever the option, an encoding failure inside a builtin must remain an
ordinary AutoLISP error that vl-catch-all-apply can catch. It was caught
here only because the call was outside the probe's catch-all; check that it
is not a host condition that escapes.

* Measured (2026-11-03, catalogue cases 535-545)

=pcat-roundtrip= writes a string with write-line through =(open f "w")=
(the product's default encoding), then reads the file back with read-char:

| case | string written | AutoCAD 2022 | BricsCAD V25 Windows | BricsCAD V26 macOS | clautolisp --dialect autocad |
|------+----------------+--------------+----------------------+--------------------+------------------------------|
| 535 | é (233)  | (233 10) | (233 10) | (142 10) | (233 10) |
| 536 | € (8364) | (172 10) | (128 10) | (219 10) | (8364 10) |
| 537 | Ĭ (300)  | (44 10) ","  | (73 10) "I"  | the 8 characters \U+012C | ERROR, cannot be encoded |
| 538 | a中b     | "a-b" | "a?b" | "a\U+4E2Db" | ERROR, cannot be encoded |
| 540 | Ĭ, mode "w,ccs=UTF-8" | (44 10) | C4 AC (UTF-8) | C4 AC (UTF-8) | (300 10) (--dialect bricscad: (65279 300 10), a BOM) |

In the strings themselves, before any file is involved:
=(ascii (chr 300))= gives 44 on AutoCAD, 73 on V25 and 300 on V26.
=(vl-string->list (chr 8364))= gives (172), (128) and (8364).

So:
- No vendor signals when a character cannot be encoded.
- AutoCAD 2022 keeps the LOW BYTE of the code point (300 → 44 ",",
  8364 → 172, 中 20013 → 45 "-"). It does this already in CHR, so the file
  write only follows. That is a lossy substitution, not a replacement
  character. The LISPSYS value of the measuring profile was not recorded:
  measure again at 1/2.
- BricsCAD V25 Windows best-fits to windows-1252 (Ĭ → I, 中 → ?, € → 128).
  This is the Windows WideCharToMultiByte default, with "?" as the
  replacement.
- BricsCAD V26 macOS keeps the character in the string, and writes the
  escape \U+XXXX when the target cannot hold it. Its default write on
  macOS is not UTF-8 either: é is read back as 142, i.e. Mac Roman?
  to check.

Per-dialect defaults that follow from this: autocad = substitute the low
byte (and warn); bricscad on Windows = best-fit with "?"; bricscad on macOS
= the \U+XXXX escape. The OPTION requested above lets the user override all
three, and choose to signal.

* To do

1. DONE 2026-11-03 (see * Measured). Still open: AutoCAD at LISPSYS 1/2,
   and the AutoCAD GUI.
2. Implement the option and the global; per-dialect defaults from 1.
3. Tests, user manual (Source-file and terminal encoding), dialect warning
   entry, DEVELOP bump.

* Resolved (clautolisp / alfe 2.3.88, spec 0.14.8, 2026-11-04)

- Options =-Ereplacement R= / =--encoding-replacement R=, and per situation
  =-Efile-write-replacement R= / =--file-write-encoding-replacement R= and
  =-Eterminal-out-replacement R= / =--terminal-output-encoding-replacement R=
  (the specific one wins). R is one character, or =error=, =low-byte=,
  =best-fit=, =escape=; anything else is a usage error. Shared CLI spec
  (autolisp-cli spec.lisp), so alfe accepts them too.
- Globals =*AUTOLISP-ENCODING-REPLACEMENT*=,
  =*AUTOLISP-FILE-WRITE-ENCODING-REPLACEMENT*=,
  =*AUTOLISP-TERMINAL-ENCODING-REPLACEMENT*= (a string or a symbol, read at
  each write), seeded by the options. Departure from the sketch above: =nil=
  means "the next source decides", and the word =error= means "signal".
- The clautolisp system variable =CLAUTOLISPENCODINGREPLACEMENT=, seeded
  from the environment variable of the same name (pjb, 2026-11-04: "with a
  clautolisp-specific sysvar / envvar"), after the globals (so the option
  wins over the environment, as usual). Read internally without the
  =[clautolisp-sysvar]= notice.
- Defaults follow pjb's section 7 of error-processing-per-dialect.issue,
  which REPLACES "Under --strict the default stays to signal" above: each
  vendor dialect as its product (dialect feature =:encoding-replacement=:
  autocad low-byte; bricscad best-fit on Windows, escape on macOS / Linux);
  =--dialect clautolisp= as BricsCAD V26 macOS, the =\U+XXXX= escape;
  =--strict= and =--lax= as clautolisp. =[encoding-replacement]= registered:
  every dialect but lax warns, the first time a calling function makes a
  builtin substitute (section 7's "the first time the caller called the
  function"); annex regenerated.
- The check is done before writing (RESOLVE-ENCODING-REPLACEMENT,
  %ENCODABLE-OUTPUT-TEXT in builtins-core; CHARACTER-ENCODABLE-P and
  ENCODING-REPLACEMENT-TEXT in the reader), for WRITE-LINE, WRITE-CHAR,
  PRINC, PRIN1, PRINT, PROMPT, on files OPEN made and on the terminal. So
  =error= is an ordinary AutoLISP error vl-catch-all-apply catches, raised
  before anything is written, and SBCL and CCL behave the same (CCL used to
  substitute silently). Host stream errors were already wrapped by
  WRAP-BUILTIN-FUNCTION into AutoLISP errors; tested.
- An explicit OPEN external format carrying =:replacement= keeps its
  precedence; an explicit encoding without one gets the policy (it says
  nothing about replacement).
- alfe: forwarded to a subprocess clautolisp (option contract), installed
  in-process through the transmit bindings; ignored with a warning under
  =--autocad= / =--bricscad=.
- Decoding (reading invalid bytes) left out, as asked; no
  =-Efile-read-replacement=.
- Tests: autolisp-builtins-core/tests/encoding-replacement-tests.lisp,
  autolisp-cli/tests/cli-launch-tests.lisp. Docs: both --help, both man
  pages, clautolisp user manual "Characters the encoding cannot hold", alfe
  user manual, spec WRITE-LINE Tested Behaviour + =*** clautolisp=.
- Open vendor questions (LISPSYS 1/2, AutoCAD GUI, BricsCAD platform vs
  version, Linux, best-fit table) moved to deferred-spec-research.issue,
  "Encoding replacement", with SPEC-UNCERTAIN markers in the source.
